# PART 3 — Gradle Deep Dive

Gradle is the build system for Android. Think of it as the factory manager — it knows how to take your raw materials (code, images, config) and assemble them into a finished product (the `.apk` file).

## The two build.gradle files

```
android/
├── build.gradle         ← PROJECT level (global settings)
└── app/
    └── build.gradle     ← APP level (your actual app settings)
```

**Project-level `build.gradle`** — sets up the build environment:

```groovy
// android/build.gradle
buildscript {
  ext {
    buildToolsVersion = "34.0.0"
    minSdkVersion = 23        // minimum Android version you support
    compileSdkVersion = 34    // Android SDK version used to compile
    targetSdkVersion = 34     // Android version you're targeting
  }
  repositories {
    google()       // where to download Android dependencies from
    mavenCentral() // another package repository
  }
  dependencies {
    // The Android Gradle Plugin — teaches Gradle how to build Android apps
    classpath("com.android.tools.build:gradle:8.1.0")
  }
}
```

**App-level `build.gradle`** — configures your specific app:

```groovy
// android/app/build.gradle
android {
  compileSdk 34

  defaultConfig {
    applicationId "com.myapp"  // unique ID on Google Play Store
    minSdk 23                  // won't install on Android below 6.0
    targetSdk 34
    versionCode 1              // internal version number (integer)
    versionName "1.0"          // displayed version ("1.0.3")
  }

  buildTypes {
    debug {
      // What happens when you run during development
      debuggable true
    }
    release {
      // What happens when you build for the Play Store
      minifyEnabled true      // removes unused code
      shrinkResources true    // removes unused images/files
      proguardFiles ...       // code obfuscation rules
    }
  }
}

dependencies {
  // Libraries your app needs
  implementation "com.facebook.react:react-android"
  implementation "com.facebook.soloader:soloader:0.10.5"
}
```

## What Gradle actually does step by step

```
Step 1: RESOLVE DEPENDENCIES
       Reads build.gradle, downloads all libraries from the internet
       Stores them in ~/.gradle/caches/ (that's why first build takes forever)

Step 2: COMPILE
       Converts your Java/Kotlin code → bytecode (.class files)

Step 3: DEX CONVERSION
       Converts bytecode → Dalvik Executable (.dex)
       Android doesn't run normal JVM bytecode — it runs .dex files
       on the ART (Android Runtime)

Step 4: PACKAGE RESOURCES
       Compresses images, layouts, strings into binary format

Step 5: SIGN
       Adds a digital signature (required by Android to install)
       Debug builds use a debug keystore automatically
       Release builds need your own keystore file

Step 6: ASSEMBLE
       Zips everything into a .apk (or .aab for Play Store)
```

## The Gradle Wrapper

You never install Gradle globally. Each project ships with its own Gradle version:

```
android/
└── gradle/
    └── wrapper/
        ├── gradle-wrapper.jar        ← downloads the right Gradle version
        └── gradle-wrapper.properties ← specifies WHICH version to use
```

This is why you run `./gradlew` (Mac/Linux) or `gradlew.bat` (Windows) instead of just `gradle`. The wrapper ensures everyone on your team uses the exact same Gradle version regardless of what's installed on their computer.

