# PART 4 — React Native Core Concepts

## Components

Everything in React Native is a component. A component is a function that returns UI.

```tsx
// Simplest possible component
function MyText() {
  return <Text>Hello world</Text>;
}

// Component with props (inputs)
function Greeting({ name, age }: { name: string; age: number }) {
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Hello {name}</Text>
      <Text>You are {age} years old</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

// Using it
<Greeting name="Shivansh" age={20} />
```

## JSX

JSX is not HTML. It looks like HTML but compiles to JavaScript:

```tsx
// What you write (JSX):
<View style={{ padding: 16 }}>
  <Text>Hello</Text>
</View>

// What it actually compiles to:
React.createElement(View, { style: { padding: 16 } },
  React.createElement(Text, null, "Hello")
)
```

## The 6 Core Layout Components

```tsx
// VIEW — the box. Like a div. Does nothing by itself.
<View style={{ flex: 1, backgroundColor: 'red' }} />

// TEXT — the only component that can display text
<Text style={{ fontSize: 16, color: '#000' }}>Hello</Text>

// TEXTINPUT — keyboard input
<TextInput
  value={text}
  onChangeText={t => setText(t)}
  placeholder="Type here"
  secureTextEntry={true}  // for passwords
  keyboardType="email-address"
/>

// TOUCHABLEOPACITY — pressable, fades on press
<TouchableOpacity onPress={() => console.log('pressed')} activeOpacity={0.7}>
  <Text>Press me</Text>
</TouchableOpacity>

// SCROLLVIEW — scrollable container. Renders ALL children at once.
// Use for short lists (settings, forms)
<ScrollView>
  <Text>Item 1</Text>
  <Text>Item 2</Text>
</ScrollView>

// FLATLIST — scrollable list. Only renders what's visible.
// Use for long lists (tasks, messages, products)
<FlatList
  data={myArray}
  keyExtractor={item => item.id}
  renderItem={({ item }) => <Text>{item.name}</Text>}
/>
```

