# PART 5 — React Hooks In Depth

Hooks are functions that give your component superpowers.

## useState — remember values

```tsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
//     ↑ value  ↑ setter  ↑ initial value

// Reading: just use count
<Text>{count}</Text>

// Writing: always use the setter, never modify directly
setCount(5);          // set to 5
setCount(c => c + 1); // set based on previous value (safer)

// WRONG — never do this:
count = 5; // this won't trigger a re-render
```

## useEffect — run code at specific times

```tsx
// 1. Run ONCE when component mounts (like componentDidMount)
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, []); // ← empty array means "run once"

// 2. Run when a value changes
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('username changed to:', username);
}, [username]); // ← runs whenever username changes

// 3. Run on every render (rarely used)
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('rendered');
}); // ← no array at all

// 4. Cleanup function (runs when component unmounts)
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => tick(), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(timer); // ← cleanup
}, []);
```

## useRef — value that doesn't cause re-renders

```tsx
// Use case 1: store a value without triggering re-render
const count = useRef(0);
count.current++; // changing this doesn't re-render

// Use case 2: animations (Animated.Value lives in a ref)
const opacity = useRef(new Animated.Value(1)).current;

// Use case 3: access a DOM element (focus a TextInput)
const inputRef = useRef<TextInput>(null);
<TextInput ref={inputRef} />
inputRef.current?.focus(); // programmatically focus it
```

## useContext — share data without props

```tsx
// 1. Create the context
const ThemeContext = createContext<'light' | 'dark'>('light');

// 2. Provide it high up in your tree
function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      <MyScreen />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}

// 3. Consume it anywhere inside the tree
function MyScreen() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
  // theme === 'dark'
}
```

