# PART 10 — Node.js and Express

## What Node.js actually is

Node.js is JavaScript that runs on your server instead of in a browser. The key difference:

```
Browser JS:
  - Can access the DOM (document, window)
  - Cannot access the file system
  - Cannot listen on network ports
  - Sandboxed for security

Node.js:
  - No DOM (no document, no window)
  - CAN access the file system (fs module)
  - CAN listen on ports (net, http modules)
  - Full system access
```

## Express — the web framework

Express handles HTTP requests. Every request goes through a pipeline of middleware functions:

```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Middleware runs on EVERY request (in order)
app.use(express.json());      // parses JSON body
app.use(express.urlencoded()); // parses form data
app.use(cors());              // adds CORS headers

// Route handlers run on SPECIFIC requests
// Structure: app.METHOD(PATH, ...MIDDLEWARE, HANDLER)
app.get('/users', async (req, res) => {
  // req = incoming request
  // res = outgoing response
  const users = await db.User.findAll();
  res.status(200).json({ users }); // send JSON response
});

app.post('/users', async (req, res) => {
  const { name, email } = req.body; // body was parsed by express.json()
  const user = await db.User.create({ name, email });
  res.status(201).json({ user });
});

app.get('/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const { id } = req.params;    // from the URL: /users/123
  const { page } = req.query;  // from ?page=2
  const user = await db.User.findByPk(id);
  res.json({ user });
});

app.listen(8000, () => console.log('Server running on port 8000'));
```

## Middleware explained

Middleware is just a function with three parameters: `(req, res, next)`:

```js
// A middleware that logs every request
function logger(req, res, next) {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} at ${new Date()}`);
  next(); // MUST call next() or the request stops here forever
}

// A middleware that checks authentication
function protect(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization;
  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token' });
    // Notice: no next() here — request is rejected
  }
  req.user = { id: 'abc123' }; // attach data for next middleware
  next(); // pass to the next middleware or route handler
}

// Chain them together
app.get('/tasks', logger, protect, async (req, res) => {
  // Only reaches here if protect() called next()
  console.log(req.user.id); // 'abc123' — set by protect middleware
  res.json({ tasks: [] });
});
```

