# PART 12 — Redis (your session store)

Redis is an in-memory key-value store. Think of it as a dictionary that lives in RAM — extremely fast but not for complex queries.

```
PostgreSQL                     Redis
──────────────────────────     ──────────────────────────
Stores data on disk            Stores data in RAM
Survives server restarts       Gone if server restarts (by default)
Complex queries (SQL)          Only key-value lookup
Slower (disk I/O)              Extremely fast
Good for: user data, tasks     Good for: sessions, caches, rate limits
```

Your app uses Redis like this:

```js
// On login — store the session
const key = `app:${userId}:${hashedToken}`;
await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify({ userId, loginAt: Date.now() }));
await redis.expire(key, 86400); // expires in 24 hours

// On every protected request — check the session
const session = await redis.get(key);
if (!session) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Session expired' });

// On logout — delete the session
await redis.del(key);
```

