# PART 13 — The Android Build Pipeline in Full Detail

When you run `npx react-native run-android`, here's every step:

```
Step 1: Metro Bundler starts
        Reads index.js → follows all imports → bundles into one JS file
        This is your "business logic" bundle

Step 2: Gradle builds the Android app
        Compiles Java/Kotlin native code
        Packages your app's resources (icons, strings, etc.)
        Copies the Metro JS bundle INTO the APK

Step 3: APK is assembled
        Signed with a debug key
        Pushed to the emulator via ADB (Android Debug Bridge)

Step 4: App launches on emulator
        Android runs the Java entry point (MainActivity.java)
        MainActivity starts the React Native runtime
        The JS bundle is loaded and executed by the Hermes engine
        Your React component tree renders to native Android views
```

## The AndroidManifest.xml

Every Android app must declare its permissions and components here:

```xml
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <!-- Permissions your app needs -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

  <application
    android:name=".MainApplication"
    android:label="@string/app_name"    <!-- the app name on the phone -->
    android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"  <!-- the app icon -->
    android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

    <!-- The main screen of your app -->
    <activity android:name=".MainActivity">
      <intent-filter>
        <!-- This makes it the app that launches when tapped -->
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
      </intent-filter>
    </activity>

  </application>
</manifest>
```

