# PART 14 — iOS Build Pipeline

iOS works differently from Android. Instead of Gradle, it uses:

```
CocoaPods     → dependency manager (like npm but for iOS libraries)
Xcode         → the IDE and build tool
xcodebuild    → the command-line build tool

Your Podfile (ios/Podfile) lists native iOS dependencies:
  pod 'React-Core'
  pod 'RNSvg'         ← react-native-svg's iOS part
  pod 'RNSVG'

Running `npx pod-install`:
  → reads Podfile
  → downloads all pods (native iOS libraries)
  → generates Podfile.lock (like package-lock.json)
  → creates .xcworkspace (the Xcode project you open)
```

## Why `pod install` is needed after installing packages

```
npm install react-native-svg
        ↓
Downloads the JS part + the native iOS part
BUT the native iOS part isn't compiled yet

pod install
        ↓
Reads the new dependency
Downloads the Xcode project files
Links them into your iOS build

Without pod install → Xcode doesn't know the native library exists
```

