# PART 15 — Key Things to Remember

## The mental model

```
React Native is NOT a web app wrapped in a browser.
React Native is NOT compiling JS to Java/Swift.

React Native runs JavaScript in a JS engine (Hermes),
which talks to native platform code via a bridge.
Your JS code says "create a box" and the bridge
tells Android/iOS to create a real native view.
```

## The most common beginner mistakes

```
1. Modifying state directly
   WRONG:  user.name = 'Alice';
   RIGHT:  setUser({ ...user, name: 'Alice' });

2. Async in useEffect without handling it
   WRONG:  useEffect(async () => { ... }, []);
   RIGHT:  useEffect(() => { const fn = async () => {}; fn(); }, []);

3. Missing dependency arrays
   useEffect(() => { load(userId); }); // runs on EVERY render
   useEffect(() => { load(userId); }, [userId]); // runs when userId changes

4. Mutating arrays
   WRONG:  tasks.push(newTask); setTasks(tasks);
   RIGHT:  setTasks([...tasks, newTask]);

5. Using index as key in FlatList
   WRONG:  keyExtractor={(_, index) => index.toString()}
   RIGHT:  keyExtractor={item => item.id}
   (index keys cause bugs when list items reorder)
```

## The complete stack your app uses

```
React Native (UI framework)
    ↕ communicates via bridge
Android (Java/Kotlin) / iOS (Swift/ObjC)
    ↕ HTTP requests
Express.js (web framework)
    ↕ queries via Sequelize ORM
PostgreSQL (relational database — stores users, tasks)
    ↕ key-value lookups
Redis (session store — stores login sessions)
```

Every layer has one job. React Native draws the UI. Express handles HTTP. Sequelize talks to Postgres. Redis stores sessions. They're all connected but each one is replaceable — you could swap Express for Fastify, or Postgres for MySQL, and the other layers wouldn't care.